          "type": "turn",
          "question": "No chest pain at all, but I do get a bit puffed walking up the hill lately. Is that just age?",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine by the rule table, but get it checked soon.** The tool found no emergency pattern. That is not a clearance. Breathlessness that is new and comes on with effort is something a doctor should look at in the next few days, not something to wait out.\n\n**Is it just age?** I can't say that from the guidance I have. Nothing I retrieved says age explains new breathlessness on exertion. Older adults can have heart attacks that show up as breathlessness rather than chest pain. The guidance says breathlessness can be the only sign, that \"the absence of classic pain is not reassuring,\" and that this is more common in older adults, women, and people with diabetes. Only a clinician can judge whether age is the cause.\n\n**What the guidance says needs ruling out:**\n- **Heart causes.** Breathlessness out of proportion to effort is a reason for medical review before strenuous activity (physical activity guidance).\n- **Lung and chest infections.** Breathlessness is listed as a sign of a possible complication of a respiratory infection needing assessment (common cold guidance). People with chronic heart or lung disease, diabetes, or a weakened immune system should be assessed earlier.\n- **Other causes.** The tool also suggested migraine, osteoarthritis and an underactive thyroid. These don't fit breathlessness well and I can't support them from the passages, so I'd treat them as unconfirmed. I could not check other common causes, such as anaemia, because my search tool ran out of budget.\n\n**Get emergency help immediately (call emergency services) if you have:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that starts suddenly\n- Chest pain or pressure with the breathlessness, or pain spreading to the jaw, back or upper belly\n- Trouble speaking in full sentences, or needing to pause for breath between words\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face or fingertips\n- Fainting, collapse, confusion, or extreme drowsiness\n\n**See a doctor within a few days, sooner if it is getting worse**, if you have breathlessness with effort that is getting worse, new ankle swelling, a cough or fever, or you feel more tired than usual.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
